+### Proposed: one skill builds guards - session-coherence-skill-generator
+
+*Proposed 2026-10-04 by an entropy-assessment run, awaiting Justin.*
+
+**Context**: Two skills each build guards, with different templates: `skills/docs-first-planning-assessment/` Phase 2 (Step 7, "Design the docs-first delta guard") and `skills/session-coherence-skill-generator/`. `INTENT.md` ("The guard lifecycle: four distinct tools") names only the assessment workflows as the guard generator. `skills/entropy-assessment/` routes no shape to the session-coherence generator, so its non-docs-first routes have no guard builder at all. No entry here records adopting the session-coherence generator; the entry below adds a mode to it. The generator still carries references to another project ("FlowBook").
+**Proposal**: Make `skills/session-coherence-skill-generator/` the only skill that writes guards. `docs-first-planning-assessment` and `entropy-assessment` supply analysis and checks to it; `entropy-assessment` routes young repos to its bootstrap mode; `guards-integrator` takes its output. If accepted, `INTENT.md`'s lifecycle names the generator as the guard generator; that edit waits for this decision.
+**Impact**: One template to maintain and fix. Every route through the front door ends in a guard builder. Removes the risk that two repos assessed the same week get differently shaped guards.
+
+---
+
 ### Session-coherence generation should bootstrap young repos before generating guards
 
 **Context**: A repo can outgrow chat/local memory before it has enough repeated workflow history to justify a dedicated session-coherence guard. The previous `session-coherence-skill-generator` mostly targeted repos that already had durable context surfaces, and treated very small repos as a case where the skill should simply suggest a lightweight `TODO.md` or `AGENTS.md` first.
